4. BOOST NEVER AN ExpenseSource; must be cuttable and below-floor — REFUTED, holds up (with one
   backlog note).
   Confirmed the boost is not a member of `s.ExpenseSources` anywhere. Diffed
   internal/services/retirement/engine/stepper.go against master (895d509): `plannedLivingExpenses :=
   st.CurrentLivingExpenses + LivingSpendingBoostAtMonth(...)` is fed into `floorAdjustedLiving`, which
   applies the guardrail multiplier AND the absolute floor to the COMBINED (base+boost) figure — so the
   boost is proportionally cut like the rest of living spending and can be driven to the floor along
   with it; `st.CurrentLivingExpenses` is seeded from `baseLivingExpensesAtMonth` (boost-excluded) so the
   boost never compounds into the evolving base (this is exactly mutation (a) from the checker-tests
   brief; the diff shows the fix is present).
   Side observation (BACKLOG, not a FAIL — out of the "new surfaces" scope): the pre-existing,
   unrelated `engine.CalculateExpenseBreakdown` (used only by the legacy Monte-Carlo-tab "adaptive
   spending" crash-buffer card, monte-carlo.html:264-285, not the new spending-optimizer surfaces) calls
   the same `livingExpensesAtMonth` helper for its "Essential"/"not marked discretionary" total. Since
   that helper now includes the boost (it didn't on master), a user's boost silently rides into that old
   card's "Expenses not marked discretionary" figure. The card's copy is already careful ("does not
   define protected essentials"), so nothing false is claimed, but it's a real, untested behavior change
   to an old surface via a shared helper — worth a follow-up regression test, not grounds to fail this task.

5. WORST PATH IS ONE COHERENT PATH — REFUTED, holds up.
